theory.intro:
A **real number** is any number that can be shown as a point on the number line. Real numbers come in two families: **rational** numbers, which can be written as a fraction $\frac{a}{b}$ of integers with $b\neq0$, and **irrational** numbers, which cannot.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8.6,5.7);
\node at (4.3,5.25) {Real numbers};
\draw (0.35,0.35) rectangle (4.2,4.8);
\node at (2.275,4.35) {Rational};
\node[font=\small, align=center, text width=3.3cm, inner sep=0pt] at (2.275,3.25) {fractions,\\terminating and\\recurring decimals,\\percentages};
\draw (0.7,0.6) rectangle (3.85,2.1);
\node at (2.275,1.35) {Integers};
\draw (4.55,0.35) rectangle (8.25,4.8);
\node at (6.4,4.35) {Irrational};
\node[font=\small, align=center] at (6.4,2.7) {decimals that\\never stop and\\never repeat};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

